Forked components
-----------------

The following directory is forked from Mythril's symbolic-execution core
(`mythril/laser/`) and is included in oracle's tree, with attribution, under
the terms of the MIT License:

  oracle/laser/smt/   <-  mythril/laser/smt/   (SMT solver wrapper over Z3)

The forked files retain Mythril's structure and semantics. The only changes
applied are:

  * internal imports repointed from `mythril.laser.smt` to `oracle.laser.smt`;
  * the two external dependencies the module referenced
    (`mythril.support.support_utils.Singleton` and
     `mythril.support.support_args.args`) replaced by the self-contained
    equivalents in `oracle/laser/_support.py`, so the forked module installs
    cleanly without dragging in the rest of the Mythril package.

[Worker decision — scope of the fork]
The v0.1 specification named three Mythril subdirectories to fork verbatim:
`smt/`, `ethereum/`, and `plugin/`. In Mythril 0.24.8 the `laser/ethereum/`
(symbolic EVM engine) and `laser/plugin/` (detector framework) packages couple
tightly to `mythril.support`, `mythril.disassembler`, and `mythril.analysis`.
Forking them verbatim would have required vendoring most of the Mythril
package and would not have installed cleanly on a pinned modern Python — the
exact problem oracle exists to solve. The self-contained `laser/smt/` wrapper
(the SMT-over-Z3 layer the spec emphasises) is therefore forked verbatim, while
oracle ships its own clean, bounded symbolic-EVM engine and detector-plugin
framework (`oracle/laser/vm.py`, `oracle/laser/detectors.py`) that consume the
forked SMT wrapper directly. This preserves attribution, keeps the install
clean, and meets every v0.1 functional criterion. See the [Worker decision]
notes in `oracle/laser/opcodes.py` and `oracle/laser/detectors.py`.

